    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72500.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Sure, fair enough. You know, one of the things that set this country apart I've found is that you mentioned our European colleagues and partners, they do tend to lean more towards overregulation and particularly letting government take that sort of lead role. And obviously that happens here a lot, but there's so many industries that regulate themselves. and the government lets them do that. I mean, even financials, I'm not talking as an accountant here, even financial statements, when a public company issues them, the SEC requires that they're audited and they get audited by, you know, big accounting firms, the government is not auditing them unless there's a problem. Do you foresee that as a potential avenue to regulate AI for there to be more private oversight, more auditing. I'm not saying like the big four accounting firms like Deloitte or Pricewaterhouse would maybe they would perform these audits. But do you see that as potentially the future or do you think AI is going to require just straight out more government auditing to make sure that people are in compliance?",
